    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          You can train for a marathon on a treadmill, and many people do most of their training
          indoors. The belt handles easy runs, tempo work and controlled hills extremely well. What
          it handles badly is everything that makes a road marathon a road marathon — downhills,
          turns, wind, self-pacing and the sheer mental length of a long run with nothing changing.
          The plan below works with those strengths and patches the gaps.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Yes, you can train for a marathon on a treadmill.</strong> The physiology of
            running at a given effort is the same indoors and out, and a treadmill can carry most
            of your weekly mileage. Five adjustments make the difference between treadmill
            training that transfers to race day and training that does not:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Use about 1% incline for steady and faster running</strong> if you want the
            effort to resemble running outdoors on a still day.</li>
            <li><strong>Cool the room and put a fan on yourself.</strong> Heat, not the belt, is
            why treadmill long runs feel so hard.</li>
            <li><strong>Break long runs into segments</strong> with something changing every
            mile or two, and rehearse your race-day fuel and fluids.</li>
            <li><strong>Do some running outdoors</strong> — especially some long runs and some
            downhills — before race day.</li>
            <li><strong>Make sure the machine is up to it.</strong> Two- and three-hour runs at
            running pace are a long continuous load for a home treadmill's motor, belt and
            controller.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            The sections below take each of those in turn, then sketch what a general week can look
            like. They are not a substitute for a training plan written for you; a coach or a
            reputable published plan supplies the progression, and this page helps you carry it
            out on a belt.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="one-percent" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. The 1% Incline Convention, and Pace Displays</h2>
<p>
            On a treadmill you are not moving through the air, so you do no work against air
            resistance. At easy paces that makes little difference. At steady and faster paces the
            gap grows, and the long-standing convention is to set a 1% incline to roughly
            compensate. It comes from a single, small 1990s study, and it is a useful default rather
            than a law — our
            <a href="/treadmill-vs-running-outside/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill vs running outside</a>
            page explains where it came from and its limits.
          </p>
          <p>
            For marathon training, a practical approach is: easy runs at whatever grade feels
            natural, 0 to 1%; marathon-pace and tempo work at 1%, so the effort you learn is closer
            to the effort you will feel outdoors. It does no harm either way.
          </p>
          <p>
            The bigger issue for marathon pacing is the display itself. Treadmill speed readouts
            are calibrated to varying standards, and the belt may be running somewhat faster or
            slower than the console says — more often on older or cheaper machines. A runner who
            does every marathon-pace session at "8:00 per mile" on a belt that is actually a little
            slow will find race pace harder than expected.
          </p>
          <p>
            The fix is to train effort as well as numbers. Run some marathon-pace sessions
            outdoors with a GPS watch, or on a measured track, and note how the effort and
            breathing compare with the same displayed pace indoors. Treat your treadmill as
            consistent with itself — excellent for "same as last week" — rather than as precisely
            correct. Our <a href="/treadmill-pace-and-speed-chart/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">pace and speed chart</a>
            converts between mph and minutes per mile for setting the belt.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="long-runs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. Surviving Long Runs on a Treadmill</h2>
<p>
            The long run is the defining session of marathon training and the one treadmills make
            hardest — not physically, but mentally. Two hours facing a wall, with a display counting
            tenths of a mile and a stop button within reach the whole time, is a test of patience
            that an outdoor route never sets.
          </p>
          <p>
            The runners who manage it well tend to do the same few things.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Break it into segments.</strong> A 16-mile run is daunting; four 4-mile blocks
            are not. Give each block a slightly different setting — a small change in incline
            between 0 and 2%, or a short stretch at marathon pace — so that something changes every
            few miles. Many runners find a long run passes faster with a small variation every mile
            than as one unchanging effort.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Plan the entertainment.</strong> A film or a long series of episodes is the
            standard answer for a reason: it takes your attention off the display. Podcasts and
            audiobooks suit people who find screens distracting while running. Some runners cover
            the console's distance readout entirely.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Split the run if you need to.</strong> Running part of a long run outdoors and
            finishing it on the treadmill — or the reverse — is common and perfectly sensible. So
            is starting outdoors and retreating to the belt when the weather turns.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Accept that it may feel harder.</strong> Many runners report that a treadmill
            long run feels harder than the same distance outdoors. Usually that is heat and
            monotony, not the belt. Fixing the heat (below) solves a surprising amount of it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Do not hold the rails when tired.</strong> Late in a long run the temptation to
            rest a hand on the console is strong. It changes your posture and stride, and if you
            need it to stay on the belt, slow down or stop.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="fuel-and-fluids" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Fuel and Fluids: The Logistics</h2>
<p>
            One genuine advantage of a treadmill long run is that your kitchen is the aid station.
            That makes it an excellent place to practise race-day fuelling, as long as you handle
            the logistics safely.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Rehearse what you will use on the day.</strong> Long runs are where you find
            out whether a particular gel, drink or chew sits well with you at running effort, and
            how often you can manage it. Practise with the products and timing you plan to race
            with. The specifics — how much carbohydrate, how often, how much to drink — vary a great
            deal between individuals and are a question for a sports dietitian rather than a
            treadmill site.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Set everything up before you start.</strong> Bottles in the console holders,
            gels on the console tray or a small table beside the machine within easy reach, towel
            over a rail. You should never need to turn around, bend down or step off to reach
            anything.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Slow down to drink and eat.</strong> Drop to an easy jog or walk for the few
            seconds it takes to open a gel or drink from a bottle, then build back up. Fumbling a
            wrapper at marathon pace is how people drift to the back of the belt, and dropping
            something onto a moving belt is a genuine hazard — stop the belt to retrieve it rather
            than reaching down.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Expect to sweat more indoors.</strong> Without airflow, you will often lose more
            fluid on a treadmill than on a cool outdoor run of the same length. Some runners weigh
            themselves before and after a long run to get a rough sense of how much fluid they
            lose, which helps them plan drinking — but neither under-drinking nor drinking large
            amounts beyond thirst is a good idea, and that balance is individual.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Mind the electronics.</strong> Spilt sports drink on a console is a common way
            to damage buttons and displays. Use bottles with closing lids, and wipe up spills when
            you finish.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="heat" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Heat: The Hidden Variable Indoors</h2>
<p>
            Running generates a lot of heat. Outdoors, the air moving past you carries much of it
            away. On a treadmill there is no such airflow, and the room is often warm to start
            with, so your body has to work harder just to stay cool. For a 30-minute run that is a
            minor nuisance. For a two-hour long run it can be the single biggest factor in how the
            session feels.
          </p>
          <p>
            The signs are familiar: heart rate drifting steadily upward at an unchanged pace,
            sweat pouring off without cooling you, and a pace that felt easy at mile two becoming a
            struggle by mile ten. Some heart-rate rise over a long run is normal; a warm room makes
            it much larger.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Use a real fan.</strong> Built-in console fans are generally weak and aimed
            awkwardly. A standalone fan at chest height, pointed at you, makes a large difference.
            Two fans are not excessive for long runs.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Cool the room.</strong> Open a window, run early, or put the treadmill in the
            coolest room you can. A garage in summer or a small spare room with the door shut can
            be uncomfortably hot by the second hour.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Treat warning signs seriously.</strong> Dizziness, nausea, confusion, chills or
            a headache during a hot session are reasons to stop, cool down gradually and drink —
            not to push through. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-warm-up-and-cool-down/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">warm-up and cool-down guide</a>
            covers what to do if you feel lightheaded when you stop.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you train by heart rate, expect indoor readings at a given pace to run higher than
            outdoors in cool weather. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-heart-rate-zones/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">heart rate zones guide</a>
            explains drift and why chasing a fixed number over a long run means slowing down for no
            good reason.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="specificity-gap" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. The Downhill Gap, and What Else a Belt Cannot Teach</h2>
<p>
            A road marathon asks a few things of you that a treadmill cannot rehearse. None of them
            is a reason to avoid the belt; all of them are reasons to do some running outdoors.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Downhills.</strong> Most home treadmills do not decline at all. Running
            downhill loads the front of the thighs while they lengthen to brake each step, and that
            is the kind of work that leaves legs wrecked in the final miles of a hilly marathon if
            they have not been prepared for it. Even an apparently flat course has gentle descents.
            If your race has meaningful downhills, include outdoor downhill running in the
            preceding months, introduced gradually.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Uphills</strong> are the opposite: the treadmill is the best hill trainer most
            people will ever own. If your course is hilly, our
            <a href="/treadmill-hill-workout/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill hill workout</a>
            sessions translate directly.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Self-pacing.</strong> On race day nothing holds your pace for you, and the crowd
            at the start will pull you faster than you intended. The ability to judge effort over
            changing terrain comes from outdoor practice.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Surface, turns and camber.</strong> Roads slope toward the gutter, corners
            demand small adjustments, and asphalt is firmer than most treadmill decks. Many runners
            find that hours on a cushioned deck leave their legs less prepared for the pounding of
            a road marathon than they expected. Our guide to
            <a href="/treadmill-cushioning-explained/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill cushioning</a>
            covers what deck suspension does and does not do.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Weather.</strong> Racing in wind, rain, cold or warm sun is easier if you have
            trained in it at least occasionally.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="blending" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Blending Treadmill and Outdoor Running</h2>
<p>
            How much outdoor running you need depends on your circumstances and your race. These
            are common patterns rather than prescriptions.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Mostly treadmill, with outdoor long runs.</strong> Weekday runs, tempo sessions
            and hill work indoors; most long runs outdoors. This pattern keeps the session where
            specificity matters most outside, and suits people who can get out at weekends.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Mostly treadmill, with a weekly outdoor run.</strong> For people whose schedule
            or surroundings make outdoor running difficult, one outdoor run a week — ideally
            including some downhill — keeps race skills ticking over. In the final two months, try
            to make several of the longest runs at least partly outdoors.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Mostly outdoor, treadmill for bad days.</strong> The commonest pattern: run
            outdoors by default and move inside for ice, storms, darkness or extreme heat. The
            treadmill's job here is protecting consistency, which is the thing that matters most
            over a sixteen- to twenty-week build.
          </p>
          <p>
            Whatever the blend, the principle is the same: use the treadmill for precision and
            reliability, and use outdoor running for anything that resembles race day. Even runners
            who do the vast majority of their training indoors generally benefit from a few
            outdoor long runs at race-like conditions before the event.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="the-machine" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Can Your Treadmill Handle Marathon Training?</h2>
<p>
            This is the section most marathon-on-a-treadmill articles skip, and for a treadmill
            review site it is the one we know best. A two-and-a-half-hour run at steady pace is a
            very different load from a 30-minute walk, and home treadmills are not all built for
            it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Continuous horsepower, not peak.</strong> The rating that matters is continuous
            horsepower (CHP) — what the motor can sustain without overheating — not the larger peak
            figure some boxes print. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-horsepower-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">horsepower guide</a>
            suggests roughly 3.5 to 4.0 CHP for daily running, with more headroom for heavier
            runners. A machine that is adequate for three 30-minute jogs a week can be working at
            the edge of its rating for two hours straight.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Heat builds with duration.</strong> Most home motors shed heat comfortably for
            the first forty minutes or so of continuous running; beyond that, temperatures in the
            motor compartment climb, and the controller board — which is usually more
            heat-sensitive than the motor — climbs with it. Long runs are exactly the sessions that
            expose an undersized drive system. Symptoms include the belt hesitating, the machine
            cutting out mid-run or an error code that clears after a rest. Our page on a
            <a href="/treadmill-shuts-off-by-itself/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill that shuts off by itself</a>
            covers the diagnosis.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The belt and deck heat up too.</strong> Friction between the belt and deck
            rises over a long session, more so on a dry deck. Keep up with lubrication — typically
            every 40 to 50 hours of use, or as your manual specifies — which marathon training will
            reach far sooner than casual use. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-lubrication/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">lubrication guide</a>
            has the procedure.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Deck size and the console.</strong> A running belt is commonly around 60 inches
            long and 20 to 22 inches wide, because tired strides wander; our
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-size-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt size guide</a>
            explains why. Some consoles also cap the length of a single workout or roll the timer
            over at a set point; check the manual before relying on one for a three-hour run.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you are buying with marathon training in mind, our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-running/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills for running</a>
            roundup weights the drive system and deck heavily for exactly this reason.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sample-week" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. What a Treadmill Marathon Week Can Look Like</h2>
<p>
            This is a general illustration of how the session types fit together, not a training
            plan. Distances, paces and progression should come from a plan suited to your
            experience — a coach, or a reputable published plan — and most first-marathon plans run
            sixteen to twenty weeks.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Monday:</strong> rest or easy cross-training.</li>
            <li><strong>Tuesday:</strong> easy run on the treadmill, 0 to 1%, conversational
            effort.</li>
            <li><strong>Wednesday:</strong> the quality session — a tempo run or marathon-pace
            segments at 1%, or hill repeats, alternating weeks. The treadmill's pacing control is
            at its most useful here.</li>
            <li><strong>Thursday:</strong> easy run, or rest if the week is heavy.</li>
            <li><strong>Friday:</strong> rest or a short easy run.</li>
            <li><strong>Saturday or Sunday:</strong> the long run — outdoors where possible, or on
            the treadmill split into segments with fuel and a fan, at an easy effort with optional
            marathon-pace blocks later in the build.</li>
            <li><strong>The other weekend day:</strong> a short, very easy recovery run or
            rest.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Most of the week is easy, and only one session is genuinely hard besides the long run.
            That proportion matters more than any detail of the sessions. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-interval-workouts/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">interval guide</a>
            has structures for the quality day, and our
            <a href="/treadmill-workouts/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill workouts guide</a>
            explains the easy-hard balance in more detail.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you are not yet running regularly, a marathon is a long way off, and the right first
            step is building to comfortable continuous running — our
            <a href="/couch-to-5k-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">couch to 5K treadmill plan</a>
            is the place to start.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="marathon-pace" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Where the Treadmill Beats the Road: Marathon Pace</h2>
<p>
            For all its limitations, the treadmill does one marathon job better than almost
            anything else: teaching you what marathon pace feels like.
          </p>
          <p>
            Outdoors, holding an exact pace is a skill that takes years, complicated by hills,
            wind, traffic lights and GPS jitter. On a belt the pace is fixed. Running three or four
            miles at marathon pace, with a 1% grade, gives you a clear and repeatable sense of the
            rhythm and breathing that pace demands. Many runners find that memory carries over
            usefully to the first miles of a race, when adrenaline makes everything feel too
            easy.
          </p>
          <p>
            It is also the best place for runs with precise structure — marathon-pace blocks inside
            a long run, progressive runs that finish faster than they start, and tempo sessions at
            an exact speed. Combine that precision with outdoor runs for the self-pacing skill, and
            you have something close to the best of both.
          </p>
          <p>
            One caution: because the belt holds pace for you, it can hide fatigue. On the road you
            would naturally slow when tired; on a belt you either keep up or stop. If a planned
            marathon-pace block is costing far more effort than usual, that is information about
            your recovery, and it is fine to lower the speed.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. The Short Version</h2>
<p>
            <strong>A treadmill can carry most of your marathon training.</strong> Use about 1% for
            steady and faster running, train effort as well as displayed pace, and use the belt's
            precision for marathon-pace and tempo work.
          </p>
          <p>
            Make long runs bearable by cooling the room, using a proper fan, breaking the run into
            segments and rehearsing your race-day fuel with everything set up within reach — and
            slow down before you eat or drink.
          </p>
          <p>
            Do some running outdoors, especially some long runs and some downhills, before race
            day. And check that your machine is built for two-hour sessions: continuous horsepower,
            a lubricated deck and a running-length belt matter more for marathon training than for
            almost any other use.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">12. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you train for a marathon on a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. A treadmill can carry most of your mileage, including easy runs, tempo work and hill sessions. Use about 1% incline for steady and faster running, keep the room cool with a fan, and include some outdoor running — especially some long runs and downhills — because a belt cannot teach self-pacing, turns or descents.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it okay to do long runs on a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is fine physically, and harder mentally. Break the run into segments with a small change every mile or two, plan entertainment, set your fuel and fluids within reach, and use a fan. Try to do at least some of your longest runs outdoors, or split them between the road and the belt.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I use an incline for marathon training on a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 1% incline is the common convention for steady and faster running, because there is no air resistance indoors. It comes from a small study and is an approximation, not a rule. For easy runs it matters little. Use extra incline deliberately for hill sessions if your race is hilly.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you fuel during a long treadmill run?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Set everything up before you start — bottles in the holders and gels within easy reach — and slow to an easy jog or walk for a few seconds while you eat or drink. Use the long run to rehearse the products and timing you plan to race with; the amounts are individual and best discussed with a sports dietitian.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why do long runs feel harder on a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Mostly heat and monotony. Without airflow your body struggles to shed heat, so heart rate and perceived effort climb over a long run. A strong fan, a cool room and breaking the run into varied segments make a large difference. The belt itself is, if anything, slightly easier than the road.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can my treadmill handle marathon training?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the motor and build. Two-hour runs are a long continuous load; look for roughly 3.5 to 4.0 continuous horsepower for daily running, a belt around 60 by 20 to 22 inches, and keep the deck lubricated. Cutting out or hesitating late in long runs often points to heat in an undersized drive system.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Training for a Marathon on a Treadmill</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">The first is about the race, the second about the session, and the last two are about the machine and the room.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Never running downhill before a hilly race</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Most home treadmills do not decline, and downhill running loads the thighs in a way uphill and flat work do not. Runners who train entirely indoors often find the uphills fine and the descents ruinous. Add outdoor downhill running gradually in the months before the race.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the displayed pace completely</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Treadmill speed displays are not laboratory instruments, and a belt that runs slightly slow will make race pace feel harder than expected. Run some marathon-pace sessions outdoors with a watch or on a track, and learn the effort as well as the number.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Doing two-hour runs in a hot, still room</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Heat drives heart rate and perceived effort up steadily over a long run, and is the main reason treadmill long runs feel brutal. A standalone fan at chest height and a cool room make a bigger difference than any other change, and dizziness or nausea mean stop.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Asking a walking treadmill to do a runner's job</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A motor rated for walking, or quoted in peak rather than continuous horsepower, can be working at the edge of its capacity for two hours at running pace. Controllers fail from sustained heat. Check the continuous rating and keep the deck lubricated before you start a marathon block.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

